<h2 id="purpose">What the State Pension is for</h2>

<p>The State Pension gives a basic income in later life. It sits beside workplace and personal pensions, which people build up through their own and their employer's contributions. The Pensions Commission describes it as the base that other retirement saving is built on. {{ m.c("pc-firm-foundation") }}</p>

<p>Most pensioners have more than one source of income. {{ m.f("share-with-state-pension") }} of pensioner units receive the State Pension, and {{ m.f("share-with-private-pension") }} receive a private pension. {{ m.f("share-state-only") }} have only the State Pension and other state benefits.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("share-state-only" | figure) }}

<h2 id="which-system">Which pension applies</h2>

<p>There are two systems.</p>

<ul>
  <li>The <strong>new State Pension</strong> is for men born on or after 6 April 1951 and women born on or after 6 April 1953. These are people who reached State Pension age on or after 6 April 2016.</li>
  <li>The <strong>basic State Pension</strong> is for people born before those dates.</li>
</ul>
<p>{{ m.c("which-system") }}</p>

<ul class="cards">
  <li>{{ m.stat("nsp-full-weekly" | figure, "The full rate for a person on the new system with the record the rules require.") }}</li>
  <li>{{ m.stat("bsp-full-weekly" | figure, "The full rate of the basic part only, for a person on the older system.") }}</li>
</ul>

{% call m.note("Why the two rates cannot be compared directly", 3) %}
<p>The basic State Pension is one part of what people on the older system receive. Many of them also get Additional State Pension, which was based on their earnings. The new State Pension replaced both parts with a single amount. So the gap between {{ m.f("bsp-full-weekly") }} and {{ m.f("nsp-full-weekly") }} does not show that people on the older system receive less in total. The averages in <a href="#what-people-receive">What people actually receive</a> show this.</p>
{% endcall %}

<h2 id="qualifying">National Insurance and qualifying years</h2>

<p>Entitlement is built up through qualifying years. A qualifying year is a tax year in which a person paid, or was credited with, enough National Insurance.</p>

<h3>The new State Pension</h3>
<ul>
  <li>A person usually needs {{ m.f("nsp-min-qualifying-years") }} qualifying years to get any new State Pension.</li>
  <li>A person whose National Insurance record began after April 2016 needs {{ m.f("nsp-full-rate-years") }} qualifying years for the full rate.</li>
  <li>Each qualifying year added after 5 April 2016 adds about {{ m.f("nsp-per-qualifying-year") }} a week, up to the full rate.</li>
</ul>
{{ m.sourceNote("nsp-full-rate-years" | figure) }}

{% call m.note("35 years does not guarantee the full rate for everyone", 4) %}
<p>Most people who are retiring now began work before 2016. For them, the government worked out a starting amount from their record up to April 2016. {{ m.c("starting-amount") }}</p>
<p>The starting amount was reduced for any years the person was contracted out. Being contracted out meant paying lower National Insurance and building up a workplace or personal pension in place of the Additional State Pension. {{ m.c("contracted-out") }}</p>
<p>So a person with 35 or more years can be below the full rate, and a person with fewer than 35 can reach it.</p>
{% endcall %}

<h3>The basic State Pension</h3>
<p>The number of years needed for the full basic State Pension depends on date of birth and sex. {{ ("bsp-30-years" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("bsp-30-years") }}</p>

<h3>National Insurance credits</h3>
<p>People who are not paying National Insurance can get credits in some situations, for example when they are unemployed, ill, or caring for a child or an adult. Some credits are automatic. Others have to be claimed. {{ m.c("ni-credits") }}</p>

